191 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 128 0 R
/T (The clearest expression of the change is the rush to move oil around the Strait of Hormuz rather than through it. The February closure turned a long-discussed hedge into an urgent build-out: the UAE is doubling its Hormuz-bypass pipeline capacity from 1.8 to 3.6 million barrels per day \(b/d\) by mid-2027, reportedly already about half complete, while Saudi Arabia weighs two to four million b/d of further capacity on its Red Sea corridor. \(Whether these bypasses can actually avoid interdiction is another question.\) Defence spending has moved the same way, and now shows up in procurement orders: in late July, Saab booked a roughly $1bn order for two GlobalEye early-warning aircraft from an undisclosed Middle East customer, building domestic air-defence and surveillance capability potentially in response to decimated U.S. capability due to Iranian strikes. At the strategic level, the same hedge appeared on 7 August in the Mecca Joint Defence Agreement, a NATO-style mutual-defence pact between Saudi Arabia, Turkey, and Pakistan that responds to diminished U.S. protection and may point future procurement toward Turkish and Pakistani suppliers.)
/E (The clearest expression of the change is the rush to move oil around the Strait of Hormuz rather than through it. The February closure turned a long-discussed hedge into an urgent build-out: the UAE is doubling its Hormuz-bypass pipeline capacity from 1.8 to 3.6 million barrels per day \(b/d\) by mid-2027, reportedly already about half complete, while Saudi Arabia weighs two to four million b/d of further capacity on its Red Sea corridor. \(Whether these bypasses can actually avoid interdiction is another question.\) Defence spending has moved the same way, and now shows up in procurement orders: in late July, Saab booked a roughly $1bn order for two GlobalEye early-warning aircraft from an undisclosed Middle East customer, building domestic air-defence and surveillance capability potentially in response to decimated U.S. capability due to Iranian strikes. At the strategic level, the same hedge appeared on 7 August in the Mecca Joint Defence Agreement, a NATO-style mutual-defence pact between Saudi Arabia, Turkey, and Pakistan that responds to diminished U.S. protection and may point future procurement toward Turkish and Pakistani suppliers.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
