204 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 130 0 R
/E (Continued attacks on vessels near Oman in July and August confirm that the strait remains dangerous ahead of any deal. And the Houthi blockade of Saudi-associated vessels is now spreading the risk into the Red Sea, deploying missiles and drones along the approach, with tankers carrying Saudi cargo turning back. U.S. maritime authorities have issued a standing advisory covering the Red Sea, Bab el-Mandeb, Gulf of Aden, and Arabian Sea. Marine war-risk cover and rerouting costs stay elevated, and pre-deal transit schedules should be treated as provisional. Additionally, Saudi-linked shippers now face a two-sided squeeze and should price Red Sea rerouting, convoy timing, and war-risk cover into both corridors, not just the Gulf.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
205 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 130 0 R
/E (SAUDI AIRSPACE IMPACTED)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
206 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 130 0 R
/E (On 4 August, a Houthi drown strike on Najran airport suspended operations there, the second disruption to southern Saudi aviation since a missile-and-drone barrage closed four airports on 13 July. The conflict now reaches Saudi aviation and logistics, in addition to shipping. Companies operating in southern Saudi Arabia should build force-majeure and rerouting contingencies into their supply lines and expect intermittent airport and airspace closures.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
207 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 130 0 R
/E (STRIKES REACH SAUDI ENERGY INFRASTRUCTURE)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
208 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 130 0 R
/E (The war is hitting Saudi hydrocarbon assets directly. Houthi missiles and drones struck the Aramco refinery at Jizan in late July and again on 9 August, when a fire was extinguished with no casualties; the group also claimed strikes on Yanbu and, on 27 July, on the Abqaiq processing complex. The attacks lift insurance, security, and business-continuity costs across Saudi energy operations, and firms with Saudi hydrocarbon exposure should treat physical-security and force-majeure risk as elevated rather than remote.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
